{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790529950559,"sessionID":"ses_f1c5a4875ffeR5KO5Iu7pV8EKt","part":{"id":"prt_0e3e62fcd001LM7Rb9Yhlayiqq","messageID":"msg_0e3e62d4d001MjafHDi3axDrJw","sessionID":"ses_f1c5a4875ffeR5KO5Iu7pV8EKt","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I can see the key issue! The `emergency_strategy()` in the global strategy includes `proactive_sleep_strategy` and `emergency_strategy`, and the Elbereth engraving as a \"last resort\" at the very bottom. But it only triggers when:\n- HP < max/5 or HP < 5 (critically low)\n- Every other emergency option already exhausted\n\nThe problem is that for most seeds, the bot reaches critical HP too late, after already dying to monsters or starving.\n\nThe key insight from the comment at lines 1635-1642:\n> hypothesis: many runs die in melee at low XP (Xp5-7) across all four identities. This\n> Elbereth last resort sits at the very bottom of emergency_strategy -- below the healing\n> cast, healing potion, fruit juice and prayer -- so it only fires when the Healer is at\n> critical HP (< max/5 or < 5) with every other emergency option already exhausted, i.e. a\n> near-certain death. Engraving Elbereth in the dust scares off the common early attackers\n> (most animals, humanoids, etc.), and waiting on it lets HP regenerate, converting otherwise\n> terminal combat deaths into survival == more XP == more score.\n\nSo the bot DOES have Elbereth engraving as an emergency measure, but it's too late - it only fires when death is near-certain.\n\nMy hypothesis: **If the bot proactively engraves Elbereth earlier (not just as a last resort in emergency_strategy), many early deaths would be prevented, allowing more seeds to reach deeper levels and higher scores.**\n\nThe focused change would be to add early Elbereth engraving as a separate strategy, or to move it earlier in the preemption chain in `global_strategy()`.\n\nLet me think about the best way to implement this. I could:\n\n1. Add a new strategy that engraves Elbereth early (e.g., as soon as the bot is on Dlvl 1 and has a free turn)\n2. Move the existing Elbereth engraving earlier in the preemption chain\n3. Add a condition to engrave Elbereth when first reaching Dlvl 1, or after a certain number of turns\n\nLet me look at what the best approach would be. The key is to engrave Elbereth early, before monsters close in.\n\nActually, let me re-read the global_strategy to understand the preemption order better. The strategies are preempted in order, meaning earlier strategies take priority. Wait, actually, let me re-read the preempt function to understand the priority direction.\n\nLooking at the `preempt` method in strategy.py:\n```python\ndef preempt(self, agent, strategies, continue_after_preemption=True):\n    def f(self=self, agent=agent, strategies=strategies):\n        gen = self.strategy()\n        condition_passed = False\n        with agent.disallow_step_calling():\n            condition_passed = next(gen)\n        \n        if not condition_passed:\n            yield False\n        yield True\n        \n        assert not agent._no_step_calls\n        \n        def f2():\n            try:\n                next(gen)\n                assert 0, gen\n            except StopIteration as e:\n                return e.value\n        \n        return agent.preempt(strategies, self, first_func=f2, continue_after_preemption=continue_after_preemption)\n```\n\nAnd in global_strategy:\n```python\ndef global_strategy(self):\n    return (\n        self.current_strategy().repeat()\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.solve_sokoban_strategy()...\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.offer_corpses().preempt(self.agent, [...])\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy(),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.cure_disease().every(5),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.eat_corpses_from_ground(only_below_me=True).condition(...),\n            self.agent.eat_corpses_from_ground().every(5).condition(...),\n            self.agent.eat_from_inventory().every(5),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.follow_guard(),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.anger_peaceful_dwarf(),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.fight2(),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.engulfed_fight(),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.proactive_sleep_strategy(),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.emergency_strategy(),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.read_magic_mapping(),\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.agent.dig_down(),\n        ])\n    )\n```\n\nThe `.preempt(A, B)` means \"strategy A preempts strategy B\" - so A takes priority over B. The first strategy in the chain has the highest priority.\n\nWait, let me re-read. The `global_strategy()` chains preempts. Let me check how preempt works more carefully.\n\nLooking at strategy.py `preempt`:\n- `self` is the current strategy (the one already built up from previous `.preempt()` calls)\n- `strategies` is the list of new strategies to add\n- `agent.preempt(strategies, self, first_func=f2)` - this calls the agent's `preempt` method\n\nLet me look at the agent's preempt method:","time":{"start":1790529908685,"end":1790529950538}}}
